# Knowledge Navigation Rules

## Always start from the index

When loading knowledge for any task, start with `knowledge/index.md`. Use it to determine which chapters are relevant to the work at hand.

Never open chapter or detail files without first reading the index. The index exists precisely to make this navigation decision cheap.

## Load only what's needed

For a given task, load:
1. `knowledge/index.md` — always
2. Only the chapter `README.md` files that are relevant to the task
3. Only the detail topic pages specifically needed for this task

Do not load all chapters. Do not load chapters because they might be relevant — they must be clearly relevant to the current work.

## Chapter structure

Each chapter lives in `knowledge/{domain}/`:
- `README.md` — chapter navigation and overview (always required if the chapter exists)
- `{topic}.md` — detail pages for specific areas within the chapter (created on demand)

## Writing to knowledge

When updating knowledge during `/complete` or `/discover --refresh`:

1. Update only the specific section that changed — do not restructure the whole page
2. At the bottom of any updated section, add a traceability note:
   ```
   > Updated by: [{work-id}](../../work/archive/{path}) — {ISO date}
   ```
3. If adding a new section to a chapter, also add it to that chapter's "Subsections" list in the README
4. If adding a new chapter, add it to `knowledge/index.md`

## Marking gaps

Brownfield codebases have areas that aren't fully understood or documented. Mark gaps explicitly:

```
> ⚠️ TBD — {what is unknown and where to look to verify it}
```

Never fill a gap with a confident guess. A TBD is immediately useful — an agent downstream will know to verify before acting. A wrong fact is silently harmful.

## The index is not a dump

The master `knowledge/index.md` is a navigation tool, not a complete description of the codebase. Each chapter entry should be long enough to decide whether to open that chapter (2–4 lines), not a full summary of it.

Detail belongs in chapter README files. Deep implementation detail belongs in topic pages.

## The deploy page exception

`knowledge/deploy.md` is always a single page regardless of project size. Deployment knowledge is loaded as a unit — the navigation overhead of splitting it outweighs the token savings for most projects.

If the deploy page becomes very large (> 400 lines), consider splitting it, but prefer keeping it unified.

## Freshness markers

Every knowledge file should carry an "Updated: {ISO date}" marker near the top. When updating a file, update this marker. This makes stale sections visible at a glance.

## Preserve the TBD trail

When a TBD section is finally filled in, add a note about what resolved it:
```
> ⚠️ TBD — Resolved by [{work-id}](../../work/archive/{path}) on {date}. {One-line summary of what was discovered.}
```

This preserves the history of why the gap existed and how it was resolved.
